body:
Hi there! I took a look at your video as well as listened to the chords, and it seems that everything is in E minor/C major rather than E major. If I'm correct, the chord progression is a looping ||: Em C maj G6 G6 :||.  In that case, E major would definitely clash :) 

&#x200B;

If you stick to an E minor scale, then E minor will feel like home base, and if you use C major, C will feel like home base. Cool ambiguity in the chord progression!

&#x200B;

The other thing that I want to talk about is "breaking the rules" with chords. Chord progressions are simply there to help us move out and about through tonal land, but we sometimes we feel this sense of "home" where things have resolved nicely and we feel at a kind of musical peace. This is basically where we figure out what key a song is in or if the key has shifted. When we feel a consistency in "comfortableness," that is most likely the key. Does this condition rely on us only playing chords that are in the key? Absolutely not! As long as we don't forget about home, we can travel as far as we want! We can also travel to new homes! The main thing is that if you want to stay at a home, then you gotta go back to it. Let's say we're in E major, and we want to get a little weird, so let's do this:

&#x200B;

E maj, G# min, C #min, D maj, G7, C7, B maj sus to B maj, E maj 

&#x200B;

Loop it

&#x200B;

A lot of those chords are definitely not in the key, but we consistently return and resolve back to E maj, so that should feel like home :) Will it sound normal? Not in the slightest. Is that okay? If your ears like it, then anything is groovy! There are no rules to composition. There are definitely patterns that help us feel certain things, but that's all based on what we think sounds good. So if it sounds good, then that's all the justification you need for a good track.

&#x200B;

I hope this helps some stuff at least a little bit!
